1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:15,000
This is SharkyClips, a free desktop app that turns stream recordings into short clips. It runs on the user's own computer.

2
00:00:15,000 --> 00:00:30,000
YouTube is optional. The user connects their own channel here. The YouTube Terms and the Google Privacy Policy are linked next to the button.

3
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:40,000
The user clicks Connect YouTube. Google's sign-in opens in the browser.

4
00:00:40,000 --> 00:00:55,000
The URL shows the OAuth client ID of the project under review.

5
00:00:55,000 --> 00:01:20,000
The consent screen shows the app name SharkyClips and the two permissions it asks for: managing the user's YouTube uploads, and reading YouTube Analytics for its own uploads.

6
00:01:20,000 --> 00:01:30,000
Connected. The app shows which channel it will upload to.

7
00:01:30,000 --> 00:02:05,000
The user picks a clip, sets the details and uploads it as private. This uses the youtube scope to insert the video on the user's own channel.

8
00:02:05,000 --> 00:02:20,000
Scheduled clips can be moved or cancelled. The app only changes or deletes videos it uploaded itself.

9
00:02:20,000 --> 00:02:35,000
For the optional hook test, 48 hours after publishing, the app reads the average view percentage of that one video. This uses yt-analytics.readonly.

10
00:02:35,000 --> 00:02:55,000
To stop access, the user clicks Disconnect. The app revokes the token and deletes the YouTube data it stored. Access can also be removed on the Google account permissions page.

11
00:02:55,000 --> 00:03:00,000
All data stays on the user's computer. Privacy policy: utkualsir.github.io/sharkyclips-site/privacy.html
